Listeriosis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
High fever/severe myalgia, Unpasteurized dairy/deli meat exposure, Acute meningitis/rhombencephalitis, Pregnancy loss/stillbirth

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-024
Reason for Visit: High fever, severe confusion, and altered mental status in an elderly patient, requiring urgent neurological assessment for acute bacterial meningitis or encephalitis.
Location: Nursing Home
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly, vulnerable patient experiencing sudden high fever, profound lethargy, and a new-onset inability to communicate coherently. Urgent physical assessment is required to exclude life-threatening acute bacterial meningitis (Listeria) and initiate immediate, definitive management or hospital transfer prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Nursing staff report: "He spiked a sudden high fever, he is completely confused, and he keeps complaining that the light is blinding him."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 24 hours ago with fever and severe muscle aches (myalgia), which the staff initially attributed to a mild viral illness. He also had mild diarrhea two days prior. Over the last 6 hours, the patient developed altered mental status, severe confusion, and profound lethargy. He is now reporting excruciating photophobia. Nursing staff note he frequently consumes unpasteurized soft cheeses provided by his family.
Functional Status: Normally semi-independent with mobility aid; currently bedbound and highly lethargic.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid neurological decline and sepsis in an elderly, institutionalized patient.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/55 | HR: 115 | Temp: 39.5 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, lethargic, actively avoiding ambient room light, muttering incoherently.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals significant altered mental status (GCS 13). Marked nuchal rigidity is present (unable to touch chin to chest without severe pain). Positive Brudzinski's sign. Cranial nerve exam reveals mild facial asymmetry and early severe cerebellar ataxia (unable to perform finger-nose test), highly concerning for progressive rhombencephalitis. Cardiovascular exam reveals severe tachycardia and acute profound hypotension. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: High fever/severe myalgia, Unpasteurized dairy/deli meat exposure, Acute meningitis/rhombencephalitis, and Pregnancy loss/stillbirth (not applicable here).
Environmental Observations: Soft cheese wrappers visible on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent antibiotic use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Listeriosis (Acute Bacterial Meningitis/Rhombencephalitis) - Status: Exacerbated (Septic Shock impending)
Differentials:

Streptococcus pneumoniae Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clinically indistinguishable without CSF culture, but the specific food exposure (unpasteurized cheese) and the prominent brainstem (rhombencephalitis) signs strongly favor Listeria.

Viral Encephalitis (HSV) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of frank nuchal rigidity and shock, though it remains a critical differential requiring hospital acyclovir.

Severe Urosepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent, specific localizing meningeal signs (nuchal rigidity, photophobia, ataxia) despite the presence of systemic shock.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Impending mortality without immediate intervention)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic stigmata of acute bacterial meningitis and early rhombencephalitis (nuchal rigidity, ataxia, altered mental status) complicated by severe sepsis (hypotension, tachycardia). In an elderly patient with a clear exposure history to unpasteurized dairy, Listeria monocytogenes is the highly presumed pathogen. This is a catastrophic, time-critical medical emergency with a high mortality rate. Immediate on-site clinical evaluation was mandatory to confirm the meningeal signs, recognize the profound shock, and coordinate an immediate, emergency ambulance transfer for life-saving intravenous Ampicillin (Listeria is intrinsically resistant to standard Ceftriaxone monotherapy). This absolutely fulfills the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: IMMEDIATE call to 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport to the Emergency Department. Patient requires urgent blood cultures, lumbar puncture, and empiric IV Ampicillin + Ceftriaxone + Dexamethasone. Provide high-flow oxygen and lay the patient flat to optimize cerebral perfusion while awaiting transport.
Positive Behavioral Change: Not applicable due to altered mental status. Staff directed to compile medical records for immediate handover.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to receiving Emergency Department physician.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. Nursing staff to initiate basic life support (CPR) if the patient loses cardiac output or ceases breathing prior to paramedic arrival.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly suspected acute bacterial meningitis; the patient is critically unstable.
Clinical Course: The infection has breached the central nervous system. Mortality is extremely high in the elderly without immediate, aggressive intravenous antibiotic therapy in an intensive care setting.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The patient is in distributive shock and actively developing brain swelling. Do not attempt to sit him up or give him any oral fluids, as he will aspirate.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: (To Nursing Staff/Family): He has developed a severe bacterial infection of the brain and spinal cord lining, likely from the unpasteurized cheese. The bacteria (Listeria) is unique and requires a specific type of antibiotic immediately, which is why he must go to the emergency department via ambulance right now.
Actionable Steps: Gather all medical records and the cheese wrapper for the ED doctors. Keep the room dark and completely quiet to reduce brain stimulation.

